runsc: systemd cgroups under rootless Docker

What: when runsc runs as root of a user namespace that an unprivileged host user
owns, as under rootless Docker with the systemd cgroup driver, it asks that
user's own systemd manager for the container's scope, over the session bus at
$DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS, else $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus, else /run/user/<uid>/bus.
It places the scope under that manager's cgroup and enables only the
controllers the system delegated to it.

Why: stock runsc asks the system manager, which refuses a rootless caller
("Interactive authentication required"). The only way out is --ignore-cgroups,
which drops --memory and --cpus. runc already uses the user's own manager.

Verified: under rootless Docker 29.9.3 (systemd cgroup driver, cgroup v2,
kernel 5.15), stock runsc of this release fails to create a container with
--memory 512m with "Interactive authentication required". With this patch a
container run with --memory 512m --memory-swap 512m --cpus 1.5 gets the scope
user@<uid>.service/user.slice/docker-<id>.scope with memory.max 536870912,
memory.swap.max 0 and cpu.max "150000 100000"; allocating 1.5 GB in it is
OOM-killed (memory.events oom_kill 1), and four busy processes use 1.47 CPUs
over 20 s, throttled in 202 periods. With DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS unset,
runsc reaches the bus at /run/user/<uid>/bus, and at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus
when that is a socket, which it tries first. Under rootful Docker 29.1.3
(cgroup v2, kernel 7.0) containers still start in system.slice with their
limits.

